t and and if it is  true why the rejection  so that is an offer for for network rail  and it's at half of this dispute on the  train operating companies we've hadn't  haven't had an offer at all but it also  means that we would have to accept  massive changes to our members working  lives that at this time are unpalatable  and unacceptable the offer is also over  three years it's not an eight percent  offer for this year we didn't get a pay  deal last year it's four percent this  year and four percent next year so it's  eight percent over three years  inflation yesterday came out the retail  price index at twelve point three  percent there are predictions for next  year that it could be up to fifteen  percent  so we could what they're asking us to do  is accept the gamble that next year  our members might receive a quarter of  the rate of inflation  and of course this year when the deal  should have been struck  it was 7.1 so if you  if you add those together it could have  been well over 20 22  something in that region and they're  asking us to accept eight  yesterday there was a deal done on the  bus services in the north at 11.1  we've had a deal done in the aviation  industry of 12 and 13 this week so  they're not offering us anything really  they're offering a real term pay cut  just and a pay cut against the rate of  inflation at that time at this time  that's not acceptable to our people  sorry to interrupt there we're really  pushing for time i just want to kind of  drill down into this particular point  everyone is we saw cpi inflation at 10.1  yesterday the expectation is that that  will rise to 13 by the end of the year  who knows what it will do in the first  six months of of next year but you could  say as a likelihood it could go could go  even higher does that mean that the  rmt's negotiating positions is directly  linked to where inflation might be in  the future or where it is now i mean if  they were to offer you 10 this morning  would you take it 