ers in london out  on strike tomorrow and over the weekend  i think there is a massive response  coming from working people because  they're fed up with the way they're  being treated it's starting to look at  look a little bit like a general strike  look as things stand quite clearly i  think people understand you've got your  majority of public support on your side  and certainly the rmt and and other  unions are suggesting that you can  afford to carry on striking for months  do you accept though with the way  inflation is going there will come a  point where when your house your members  can afford you going on strike  the rest of the country can't afford you  going on strike lower paid workers can't  afford to afford you going on strike  well nobody can afford to go on strike  they don't want to be on strike they  want a settlement but they are  completely  tied into this dispute i addressed the  meeting of thousands of our members last  night online and they are totally  committed to the campaign that we've got  they understood the issues when they  voted for it and they're showing on the  picket lines that they're completely  committed to the cause we will keep  going until we get a negotiated  settlement and our members will decide  whether it's acceptable or not  lynch we're just literally about to go  into the commercial break so i thank you  for squeezing that answer and we'll  speak again  [Music]
 

